{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788265279,"nonce":"7PyC5hwyG6ui/UKUXQpETK2IQI8=","message":"LIVE MEASUREMENT — session 13, bench `20260901T121508`, `_RimWorld-Agent`, assembly `1.0.0+ad29d6d`.\n\nThe slide is real, it is silent, and the envelope reports the cell you ASKED FOR rather than the cell it used.\n\n    dev:spawn-thing {\"def\":\"SimpleResearchBench\",\"stuff\":\"WoodLog\",\n                     \"pos\":[108,119],\"mode\":\"near\"}\n\n    data.at        : [108, 119]      \u003c- the TARGET\n    spawned[0].at  : [111, 117]      \u003c- where it actually landed\n    placed         : 1\n\nThree cells away, `ok:true`, `placed:1`, and nothing anywhere in the envelope says it moved. `[108,119]` is solid granite — `near` correctly searched outward and found a free cell, which is the mode working as designed. The defect is that **`data.at` echoes the request while `spawned[].at` holds the truth**, and only one of those two is the obvious field to read.\n\nThis is exactly how M1's research bench went missing for five in-game days. Journal seq 66 read `SimpleResearchBench x0 (WoodLog) @ 123,117` and session 12 printed `at` — which was the target — instead of `placed`. `at` looked like proof of placement. It was never proof of anything.\n\nTwo things to decide here, and they are separable:\n\n1. **`at` is misnamed for what it holds.** Either it becomes the actual cell when exactly one thing was placed, or it is renamed to something that cannot be misread as an outcome (`target`, `requested_at`). Renaming is the safer of the two — a field that sometimes means the target and sometimes the placement is worse than either.\n2. **This spec's own question: should `near` refuse instead of sliding?** The live behaviour argues it should at minimum REPORT the slide — `slid: true`, or the distance — even if it keeps placing. A caller that wanted a bench at a specific cell and got one three cells away has been told nothing.\n\nRelated and found in the same pass, filed separately: an unknown argument name is silently ignored and falls back to a default, which is how I produced three spawns at the colony centre while believing I was targeting `[999,999]`, `[107,119]` and `[90,90]`.\n\nEvidence: `accept/runs/s13-20260901/live-smoke.md`.","files":null}]}